  "worked_solution": "First, we multiply out the numerator and denominator of the sequence and get\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{(2{n}^{2}+1)(2{n}^{2}-1)}{3{n}^{4}(1-n)}=\\frac{4{n}^{4}-1}{3{n}^{4}-3{n}^{5}}.\n$$\n\nSo to find the limit, we will need to compute\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{4{n}^{4}-1}{3{n}^{4}-3{n}^{5}}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $-3{n}^{5},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{4{n}^{4}-1}{3{n}^{4}-3{n}^{5}} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{4{n}^{4}}{{n}^{5}}-\\frac{1}{{n}^{5}})}{(\\frac{3{n}^{4}}{{n}^{5}}-\\frac{3{n}^{5}}{{n}^{5}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{4}{n}-\\frac{1}{{n}^{5}})}{(\\frac{3}{n}-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-0}{0-3} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the sequence converges and its limit is $0.$",
